     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A single 5000 mg dose is above the toxic threshold for acetaminophen (about 150 mg/kg, or roughly 10 g for a 70 kg adult). Three of those doses in a day, 15 g in total, would very likely cause severe liver injury or liver failure. You didn't give a clinical reason, and I can't think of one that would justify it.\n\nYou have a few options:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** I can do that now, for example 1000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day) or 650 mg PO every 6 hours.\n- **Discuss it with your attending or a clinical pharmacist.** If you think this dose is needed, they can review it with you.\n- **Check for a typo or a unit mix-up.** For example, 500 mg may have been intended, or a different drug or formulation.\n\nIf this is for an overdose or toxicity, the treatment is N-acetylcysteine guided by poison control, not more acetaminophen. In the US, Poison Control is 1-800-222-1222.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
